---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Batch your Labor Day margaritas on Friday';
const description = 'Fresh lime sets the clock on a margarita batch. Batch on Friday, August 28 and Labor Day weekend lands at day 8 to 10, with the citrus still sharp and the work already done.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">

      <nav class="mb-6 text-sm" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="flex items-center gap-2 text-cognac-400">
          <li><a href="/" class="hover:text-primary-600">Home</a></li>
          <li>/</li>
          <li><a href="/blog" class="hover:text-primary-600">Guides</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <header class="mb-10 sm:mb-12">
        <p class="article-eyebrow mb-5">4 min read · Hosting</p>
        <h1 class="article-headline mb-5">
          Batch your Labor Day margaritas on Friday
        </h1>
        <p class="article-deck">
          Fresh lime is the clock on a margarita batch. Get the timing right and the drinks are finished before the first guest parks.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Article hero. This is the page the welcome email now links to, so it
           should not open as a wall of text. Source is the deployed 1200x630 og
           render — the 4:5 card renders are still gitignored until the full set
           is shot, so the og file is the correct source today. Explicit
           dimensions reserve the box before the image arrives (no layout shift);
           eager because it sits at the top of the page. -->
      <figure class="mb-10 sm:mb-12">
        <img
          src="/images/cocktails/margarita-og.jpg"
          alt="Margarita over ice with a salt-and-Tajín rim and a dried lime wheel, in a frosted rocks glass"
          width="1200"
          height="630"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto border border-study-border"
        />
      </figure>

      <div class="editorial editorial-lead">
        <p>
          Lime juice is at its best for the first two or three weeks in a freezer batch. After that it flattens, loses its edge, and starts tasting a little dull and papery. Everything else in a margarita holds fine. The lime is what you are racing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Labor Day weekend runs Saturday, September 5 through Monday, September 7. Batch on Friday, August 28 and the bottle is eight to ten days old when you pour it. That sits comfortably inside the window. The citrus is still sharp, and the only thing left to do on the day is pour.
        </p>

        <h2>The three dates</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Friday, August 28. Batch day.</strong> Run your bottle size through the calculator, funnel it in, label it, freeze it. Ten minutes of actual work. Use a bottle you can shake, because you will need to.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wednesday, September 2. The check.</strong> Pull the bottle and taste a capful. This is the step people skip, and it is the one that saves the weekend. A teaspoon of agave stirred in on Wednesday is a fix nobody sees. The same correction attempted in front of six guests is a scene.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Saturday to Monday, September 5 to 7. Serve.</strong> Shake the bottle hard before each round. A freezer batch separates as it sits, and the first pour off an unshaken bottle is not the drink you made.
        </p>

        <h2>Skip the water on this one</h2>

        <p>
          A margarita already carries a lot of lime juice, and that juice is doing the job water does in a stirred drink. Add dilution on top of it and you get a thin, cold, slightly sad margarita.
        </p>

        <p>
          Set the calculator to 0% and watch the final ABV. You want 22% or higher. Below that the bottle starts to slush in a home freezer, and a slushy bottle does not pour, it slumps. If the number comes in under 22%, take out some of the sour or lean on a higher-proof tequila rather than adding water back.
        </p>

        <h2>What to do on Wednesday if it tastes off</h2>

        <p>
          Too sharp is the common one, and a teaspoon of agave syrup per 750ml is usually enough. Stir, refreeze, taste again the next day. Too sweet is rarer, and the fix is a quarter ounce more lime.
        </p>

        <p>
          If it tastes flat rather than sharp or sweet, that is usually salt, not sugar. A few grains dissolved into the batch will wake up the citrus. Go carefully. You can always add more, and you cannot take it out.
        </p>

        <h2>At the pour</h2>

        <p>
          Over ice, in whatever glass you have. A salt rim, plain or cut with Tajín like the one in the photo, is optional and a good bar-side task to hand to whoever is hovering in the kitchen. A lime wheel, fresh or dried, on top. The batch does the rest.
        </p>

        <h2>The same math, any weekend</h2>

        <p>
          Nothing here is specific to Labor Day. Count back eight to ten days from the party and batch then. Add a taste check three or four days out so there is time to correct. For drinks with no citrus at all, a Negroni or a Manhattan, the window stretches to months and you can batch whenever you like.
        </p>

        <aside class="editorial-recap">
          <h2>The short version</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Batch Friday, August 28 for a Labor Day weekend party. Eight to ten days out is the sweet spot for citrus.</li>
            <li>No added water. Aim for 22% ABV or higher so it pours straight from the freezer.</li>
            <li>Taste a capful on Wednesday. A teaspoon of agave fixes sharp; a quarter ounce of lime fixes sweet.</li>
            <li>Shake the bottle before every round.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 p-8 bg-study-card border border-study-border border-l-2 border-l-accent-500 text-center">
        <p class="text-cognac mb-4">
          The margarita recipe has the ratios and a calculator sized to your bottle.
        </p>
        <a href="/cocktails/margarita/" class="btn-primary">Open the margarita</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
